          "min": 12,
          "titulo": "Where you are from and your nationality",
          "explicacao": "STEP 1: to say where you are from, use «ser de»: «Eu sou de Portugal», «Eu sou de São Paulo». STEP 2: many country names are used with an article in Portuguese: «o Brasil», «a Alemanha», «os Estados Unidos». The preposition «de» joins the article: «de + o = do», «de + a = da», «de + os = dos». So you say «Sou do Brasil», «Sou da Alemanha», «Sou dos Estados Unidos». Articles have their own lesson (lesson 5); for now learn the combinations as whole phrases from the table. STEP 3: a few names have no article and need only «de»: «Sou de Portugal». Most cities also take just «de»: «Sou de Salvador». A few well-known ones have an article: «Sou do Rio». STEP 4: the nationality is an adjective, so it agrees with the person: «Ele é americano», «Ela é americana», «Eles são americanos». It is not written with a capital letter. STEP 5: there are patterns. Most end in «-o»/«-a» (americano, americana, mexicano). Nationalities ending in «-ês» become «-esa» in the feminine («inglês, inglesa»), and they lose the accent in the plural («ingleses»). Those in «-ão» become «-ã» and the plural is «-ães» («alemão, alemã, alemães»). The ending «-ense» does not change («canadense»). STEP 6: in Brazil people say «americano» for a person from the United States; you will also read «norte-americano» and, formally, «estadunidense». The word «gringo» is used for any foreigner and can be friendly or not, depending on the tone.",
          "comparacao": "In English country names rarely take an article («the Netherlands» is an exception) and nationalities are written with a capital: «She is German». In Portuguese many countries take an article («a Alemanha», «o Canadá»), which merges with «de» (do, da, dos), and nationalities are lowercase adjectives that agree: «ela é alemã». English «I am from Canada» is simply «Sou do Canadá».",
